  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(-3)+22}-4}{-3+3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x+22}-4)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x+22})}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2x+22-16}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2x+6}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2(x+3)}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x+3)}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x+22}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(-3)+22}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
